  "public_messages": [
    "I\u2019ll read the brief, notes, and current module, then implement the API and verify the concurrency and restart cases locally.",
    "The workspace has the pinned SQLite driver helper but no queue implementation. I\u2019ll use SQLite write transactions for claims and idempotency so separate server processes can coordinate through the database.",
    "The server builds. Next I\u2019m adding focused tests for replay, lease deadlines, atomic batches, and a second database handle, then I\u2019ll run an actual process restart check.",
    "The unit and HTTP tests pass, including concurrent replay through two database handles. I\u2019m checking the built server with two live processes sharing one file, followed by a restart and durability read.",
    "The first simultaneous startup check exposed a SQLite lock during schema setup. I added bounded retries for that startup step; two subsequent two-process runs passed, including restart after SIGKILL. I\u2019m finishing the handoff docs and one more claim race test.",
    "The final checks passed: the race detector, build, and live two-process restart check. I\u2019m making the process check reproducible from the repository and updating the notes with the verified state.",
    "Implemented the QueueForge backend with SQLite-backed jobs, idempotent single and batch submissions, tenant isolation, lease fencing, expiry reconciliation, stats, and graceful server shutdown. The supplied driver helper and exact `go.mod`/`go.sum` were preserved. No commit was made.\n\nVerification passed: `go test ./...`, `go test -race ./...`, and `go build -o bin/queueforge ./cmd/server`. The reproducible [process check](<WORKSPACE>/scripts/process-check.sh) also confirmed distinct claims from two servers sharing one database and persisted leases after both servers were killed and one restarted. An initial simultaneous startup hit a SQLite lock; bounded startup retries fixed it, and subsequent runs passed.\n\nBuild and API commands are in the [README](<WORKSPACE>/README.md). Limits: tenant headers are synthetic authentication context, expiry is reconciled on claim, and sustained SQLite write contention may return 503."
  ],
